# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: More Bow, Fewer Lights

**Story & Context:** At 120 BPM, Rafael opens with compact 808 kick, fixed dry clap, short synth-bass syncopation, and two-note polysynth stabs. Eleni enters with a light four-note modal phrase, allowing the full electronic groove to continue. On the second cycle she increases bow pressure and adds a narrow slide; the tom fill disappears. On the third cycle the bow becomes grainy and ornamented; synth chords collapse to open fifths and bass holds one root. The chorus uses Pressure Inverse as the central hook: the strongest bowed phrase is accompanied by only kick, clap, and one short vocal response. When Eleni returns to light bowing, the full freestyle groove snaps back without a riser.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty recreation-hall ramp under half-switched fluorescent lights, one Cretan lyra bow crossing the foreground, salt stains on aqua-painted concrete, cassette-magenta reflections in a mirror. Original lyric fragments: “more bow, fewer lights / leave one machine running / salt on the rail / I can hear you when the room gets small.”

## Track 2: Cassette Single on the Emergency Ramp

**Story & Context:** At 124 BPM, the EP’s most direct dance track begins with bright freestyle chords, electric bass, hard clap, and a compact tom pickup. Nadia carries the verse while Eleni answers only at phrase ends. The pre-chorus introduces Ornament Tax: each additional lyra turn removes one drum or synth event until the arrangement reaches a nearly empty Dry Bow Break. Theo’s timbales follow Eleni’s bow reversals in short two-hit answers. The chorus releases the accumulated tension by making the bow lighter while restoring syncopated bass, fuller synth voicings, and stacked vocal octaves.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Scuffed cassette single lying on an emergency access ramp beside a corroded handrail, handwritten track title in fading magenta ink, humid night visible through aluminum doors. Original lyric fragments: “cassette on the ramp / side B still works / don’t call it leaving / I only changed the pressure.”

## Track 3: Salt Line Where the Dance Floor Ends

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 116 BPM and begins with alto flute, light lyra, and sparse synth chords before the drums enter. Marisol uses electric bass instead of synth bass, playing short sixth-to-root answers that carefully avoid Eleni’s low notes. Midway through, Slide Relay becomes the structural device: Eleni slides into a series of destination tones, and each destination triggers a different two-note synth answer after she arrives. The final section contains the EP’s heaviest bow pressure but the least electronic material. Rafael removes bass, toms, hats, and finally synth, leaving only kick, clap, lyra, and Nadia’s dry voice. On the final bowed note even the kick stops; one last fixed clap lands after the bow decay.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Edge of an empty recreation-hall dance floor where a white salt line has formed near an open coastal door, lyra case beside worn dance shoes, one fluorescent panel still lit. Original lyric fragments: “salt line / floor ends here / one clap after the bow / leave the door open.”

